ered there. Session, model and invocation metadata are bound by the launcher record and are not restated or invented here. Assigned record path: docs/reviews/2026-09-06-sn036-decomposition-structured-opus.md.\n\nARTIFACT ACCEPTANCE VS REVIEW, AND INSPECTION. This is a decomposition review record only. It approves no Status, grants no snapshot permission, mints no carrier, adds no machine gate and orders no backfill. SN-036/SR-161/LLR-183 keep the Approved status their cells already carry — amended-but-not-re-seeded is a state the snapshot-drift arm surfaces, and the prompt records that drift as pending separate adjudication; I absorb none of it. SR-184/185/186 and TC-209/210/211 stay Drafted. This review is NOT an execution of TC-211: TC-211's result stays INCOMPLETE, and a later TC-211 execution must record its own inspector, date, subject and findings. It may legitimately take THIS record plus the supplied brief as its normal sample, which is precisely the positive input the prior inspection lacked.\n\nG1–G5 CHALLENGED AGAINST PARENT INTENT. G1 vs SN-024 (author-independence): holds — reviewer identity, rubric digest, intent sources and per-finding anchors are all reconstructible without inventing runtime facts. G2 vs SN-024 (rubric not TC-derived): the rubric is derived from SN/SR intent, not from TC-209/210/211, and it did expose a permissive shape (F1) rather than ratifying the amendment; its own coverage gap is F5. G3 vs SN-037 (counterpart preservation): the changed side is SN-036/SR-161; counterparts LLR-183 (changed) and TC-211 (changed) are handled with reasons, but TC-178 (F2) and the shipped hats.py/hats.toml prose (F4) are counterparts left undecided — resolving the IDs is not the decision SN-037 asks for. G4 vs SN-012 (proportionality): the amendment deletes a ~240-cell no-finding obligation and adds no row, which is SN-012's intent honoured; F1 is the one place it drifts back. G5: the revised procedure now points at records that exist (roster + brief + review record) instead of an absent producer, and the delivered brief is followable as written; residual observation, no finding — the procedure names \"the decomposition brief used by that review\", so a review record must embed or cite its brief to stay inspectable later; this record's brief is quoted verbatim in the sitting, so the sample is complete.\n\nAPPLICABLE HAT OUTCOMES (11 of 16, matching plan_briefs.hat_surface_for_work_item over Workstream=process, SafetyClass=spine, plus SN-012's tags=[\"process\"]).\nSECURITY — no finding. No secret is spent and no irreversible action is taken: the change edits tracked prose cells. The one irreversible-adjacent act, the needs-only snapshot re-seed through intake.py snapshot --approves, is explicitly owner-held and not performed here; authority for it is named in the ruling.\nFIRST-RUN-ADOPTER — finding F4. The amended SN-036/SR-161 are satisfiable with shipped guidance alone (hats.toml + plan_briefs + a review record), accepted evidence is preserved (TC-178 retained), and no supported caller breaks. But an adopter reading the shipped roster and hats.py is still told the record half is unbuilt.\nMAINTAINER — findings F3, F6. Two years out, the reason for the reversal is recoverable from docs/log.d/2026-09-06-sn036-owner-ruling.md and LLR-183's new discharge sentence; what would mislead is the stale present-tense cross-reference in the retained TC-211 result.\nTEST-ENGINEER — finding F2. What detects a violation of amended SR-161 is TC-211's Inspection; its ability to detect is demonstrated by the paraphrasing-child and missing-outcome counterexamples in the abnormal-input section. What it leaves unverified, stated rather than hidden: SR-161 is Approved with Inspection while its only instrument is Drafted and INCOMPLETE — openly recorded, so no separate finding, but it is the residual.\nUX-DESIGNER — no finding. No reader-facing surface changes layout or priority; the only rendered effect is SR-161's method label and the Test-verified count on the generated dashboard, and no claim is made about either.\nUX-ENGINEER — no finding. No view is added or re-rendered; the empty/huge/malformed axes are unchanged (roster absent = opt-out, unparseable = refusal, both untouched by this change).\nACCESSIBILITY — no finding. Results are stated as words (PASS / INCOMPLETE) with no colour-only signal, and the procedure prescribes reading text records, reachable by keyboard and screen reader.\nPERFORMANCE — answered \"no bound needed\", in one line as the charter permits: the change removes a per-need ~240-cell record and a checker, so operator and review cost falls; nothing here is a measurable-speed, size or provider-usage risk.\nCONSISTENCY — findings F1, F2, F4. Template/instance divergence is unaffected (hats.toml values may diverge; structure does not).\nINTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY — no finding. The amendment spans four registries plus one doc; a half-applied write leaves SN-036 amended with SR-161 stale, which no check reads, but the mitigation is the single commit and the state is nameable — the snapshot-drift arm reports amended-but-unreseeded rows, so the next reader can name where it stopped.\nPRODUCT-FITNESS — finding F1 belongs here too. The owner asked for a recorded review pass, not a guard; SR-161, LLR-183, the procedure and TC-211 deliver exactly that. F1 is the one clause that drifts back toward the refused \"record every hat everywhere\" shape by re-asserting coverage in a second row.\n\nNON-APPLICABLE ROSTER HATS, WITH PREDICATE REASON (5). UNATTENDED-OPS — applies_when tags contains \"unattended\" or \"loop\"; the declared context carries only process/spine plus SN-012's \"process\", so it does not fire, and correctly: this change touches no unattended path. CROSS-PLATFORM — requires scripts/launcher/shell; the declared tags carry none, and the change edits no script. SAFETY — requires \"safety\"; undeclared, and no physical-harm referent exists here. LEGAL — requires \"legal\"; undeclared on these parents (SN-026 carries it, not SN-012/024/036/037). DATA-PROTECTION — requires \"personal-data\"; undeclared, and no personal data is touched. Per hats.py, an undeclared field satisfies no clause, so each of these is silence by rule, not by omission.\n\nEACH CHILD'S INDEPENDENT PURPOSE, AND WHY SPLITTING STOPS. SR-161 (under SN-036) — the perspective obligation itself: applicable questions reach the brief and the sitting's record names each outcome. SR-184 (SN-024) — Critique acceptance provenance: fresh non-author session, intent-derived rubric, numbered anchors. SR-185 (SN-037) — the semantic counterpart decision the mechanical SR-162 explicitly disclaims. SR-186 (SN-012) — proportionality and the stopping decision; no other row owns it (SR-183 measures source-function complexity). LLR-183 (under SR-161) — a distinct mechanism, not a paraphrase: Hat-Refs as a resolvable cell, its severity split and the derived effective set; it survives the parent's method flip because Inspection makes an LLR exempt, not forbidden. TC-178 — supplementary mechanical evidence for LLR-183. TC-209/210/211 — one direct Inspection each, with distinct discriminating cases (missing provenance / TC-copied rubric; reference-only counterpart; paraphrasing child). Stopping rationale: no LLR is minted under SR-184/185/186 because PROCESS.md 737-744 exempts Inspection SRs and any such LLR would restate the review judgment without defining a mechanism; SR-161 is not split into a brief-side and a record-side row because one acceptance condition spans both and two rows would share one decision; TC-211 is not split per hat because the roster supplies applicability and a per-hat case would multiply cases without adding a distinct judgment. Further splitting anywhere in this chain would only relabel the same question, which is the independent-value boundary SR-186 names.\n\nVERDICT AND ANCHORS. CHANGES_REQUESTED on F1 (G4/B4), F2 (G3/B3) and F3 (G5/B5). F4, F5, F6 are advisory and route elsewhere: F4 to a follow-up outside the ruled scope, F5 to the rubric's next revision, F6 to the owner as a rule-versus-instruction conflict. Everything the owner ruling ordered was executed and I found no fabricated reading: SN-036 drops \"machine-readable\" and \"a check reports\" with need/why untouched; SR-161 re-points to roster + brief + review record with Verification=Inspection and no new cell, carrier or check; LLR-183's NOT DISCHARGED clause is closed by amendment; TC-211 and the procedure now inspect an actual review record instead of an absent producer; hats_no_finding is nowhere built. OI-86/OI-87 are recorded as ruled (a) exactly as the owner record states — no IF change, LLR-041's route already run in 13593db9, and neither row re-approves or re-amends anything."},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"79f34f90-d9d9-4e8b-b3c9-6a564e5e50ea"}
